---
id: joins-01
courseId: fullstack
title: "Why your JOIN returned 400 rows"
concepts: [inner-join, row-multiplication]
---

{% hook id="h1" estSeconds=40 character="bug" %}
Friday, 18:40. The sales report shows revenue **four times** higher than the bank does.
{% /hook %}

{% predict id="p1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/join.sql" language="sql" concepts=["row-multiplication"] %}
{% option output="100 rows" misconception="join-keeps-row-count" %}
A join can return more rows than either table has.
{% /option %}
{% option output="400 rows" correct=true %}
Each order matched four items.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e1" estSeconds=45 code="select 1" %}
One order, four items, four rows.

{% annotation line=1 %}
Four rows come out here.
{% /annotation %}

{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So the join multiplies rows?
{% /sticky %}
{% gotcha %}
Count the rows before you trust a sum.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /explain %}

{% recall id="r1" estSeconds=60 %}
{% question prompt="Why did the transaction fail?" %}
{% option text="Deadlock" correct=true %}
Right.
{% /option %}
{% option text="Typo" misconception="typo" %}
No.
{% /option %}
{% /question %}
{% question prompt="Why did the index help?" %}
{% option text="Fewer reads" correct=true %}
Right.
{% /option %}
{% option text="Magic" misconception="magic" %}
No.
{% /option %}
{% /question %}
{% /recall %}

